<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Talagunda inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Talagunda pillar inscription</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Talagunda inscription is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> verse inscription on a stone pillar at Talagunda in Shimoga district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, carved in the 5th century under the Kadamba king Shantivarma. It tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayurasharma">Mayurasharma</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> insulted by a Pallava guard at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, took up arms and founded the Kadamba kingdom, and it is a key source for early Karnataka history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talagunda-inscription.jpg" alt="Talagunda inscription" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F Kielhorn (died 1908) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:455%E2%80%93470_CE_Talagunda_Pillar_Sanskrit_inscription,_Pranavesvara_temple_ruins,_Karnataka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
